    },
    {
      "text": "1. Introduction",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        619.6008911132812,
        169.8248748779297,
        630.3486328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Computing reachable sets, i.e., the set of states reachable over a time horizon, is key to robot safety.",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        641.2686767578125,
        522.0000610351562,
        651.0214233398438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "However, reachability analysis for robots is difficult as they often (i) lack analytical models, (ii)",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        654.817626953125,
        522.0000610351562,
        664.5703735351562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "have high-dimensional nonlinear dynamics, and (iii) require reasoning over long horizons. Even if",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        668.3666381835938,
        522.0001831054688,
        678.119384765625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "analytical dynamics are available, challenges (ii) and (iii) cause nonlinear reachability tools (Bansal",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        681.9166259765625,
        522.0001220703125,
        691.6693725585938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "et al., 2017; Althoff, 2015) to become prohibitive or to suffer from excessive overapproximation",
      "bbox": [
        90.0,
        695.4656372070312,
        522.0001831054688,
        705.2183837890625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "© 2026 D. Nath, H. Yin & G. Chou.",
